\description{
Applies the same posterior predictive decision calculation used
by \code{\link[=survival_adapt]{survival_adapt()}} to subject-level data observed at one interim look.
The function does not simulate a trial or modify \code{data}. Participants who
could still be enrolled before the maximum sample size are represented
according to \code{N_total}, the observed arm counts, and \code{rand_ratio}.
}
\details{
The arm-specific maximum enrollment is
\code{N_total * rand_ratio / sum(rand_ratio)}. Potential future accrual in each
arm is its maximum enrollment minus its observed enrollment. Randomization
block information and concealed future assignment order are unnecessary:
the maximum-sample predictive calculation requires only the number of
potential future participants in each arm.

The \code{data$status} values distinguish observed follow-up: \code{"event"} for an
observed endpoint event, \code{"complete"} for event-free completion of
\code{end_of_study}, \code{"pending"} for a subject still under follow-up, and
\code{"censored"} for permanent early censoring. Pending and censored outcomes
are predictively imputed conditional on \code{time}.

\code{Sn} and \code{Fn} are scalar thresholds for this look. Expected success is
declared when the estimated probability of completed-data success among
the current participants is strictly greater than \code{Sn}. Futility is
declared when the corresponding maximum-sample probability is strictly
less than \code{Fn}. Set \code{Fn = 0} or \code{NULL} to disable futility. Exact
one-sided Monte Carlo bounds are returned as diagnostics and do not drive
either decision.

The function requires treatment assignments to perform the arm-specific
posterior and completed-data analyses. In a blinded trial, an independent
unblinded statistician or service should join the treatment assignments,
run this function, and return the aggregate decision without distributing
the subject-level input.
